# SIMPLICITY — Changelog

All notable changes to this project are documented here.
Ordered by version number. Dates use YYYY-MM-DD.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Version 2.4.62 — 2026-10-06 — pietro gerletti

`tests/run_calibrated_grid.sh` points at the session script instead of nohup.

Its header told the reader to detach the run with `nohup ... &`. The repo has
`scripts/start_simplicity_session.sh` for exactly this, and it does more than
detach: it attaches a named tmux session with the conda env activated and
`SBATCH_QOS=standard` exported. A nohup'd run would have had neither.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Version 2.4.61 — 2026-10-06 — pietro gerletti

Tests for everything that has to be settled before recalibrating, and a
correction to a claim one of them was asserting.

`test_consensus_wiring` used to ASSERT the epidemic byte-identical across the
v2.4.57 accumulator, reasoning that propensities depend on counts and not on
fitness. The propensities do; the counts do not. Re-running 6f5c185 against
037e427 with PYTHONHASHSEED pinned — controlled against the same commit on both
sides, which gives 42/42 identical — leaves 4 of 12 epidemic files differing,
argmax included. The assertion passed by configuration luck. It now reports
rather than asserts, and the docstring carries the correction.

The mechanism is still open. Traced to the first divergent event of a 5,815-event
run: an infection where both sides pick the SAME recipient and a different
parent. That is the fitness-weighted draw behaving exactly as it should, since
`Generator.choice(p=w)` and `integers(0, n)` were both measured to consume a
fixed amount of stream regardless of the weights or the bound — so fitness
cannot displace rng4, and an rng6 split for the parent draw was implemented,
tested, found to fix nothing, and reverted. How a parent swap becomes an extra
recovery thirty events later is not known. The `DISTANCE_TOL` clamp is ruled
out: reverting it alone reproduces the divergence exactly.

`test_pre_recalibration` runs the one untested configuration point (2% seeding
at N=5000) and the consensus re-check in throwaway git worktrees.
`test_fitness_epidemic_invariance` perturbs fitness by one ulp and requires the
epidemic unmoved; it passes, and its docstring states plainly that it does not
yet discriminate — it passes on the pre-fix code too, for the stream-neutrality
reason above. It is kept as a guard against a draw whose consumption later
becomes fitness-dependent.

`BACKLOG.md` was pruned to its two open technical-debt items, from 840 lines
and 70 entries. Everything that predates this session is recoverable with
`git show <pre-2.4.61>:BACKLOG.md`; the blocker entries written in this session
— the reproducibility fix, the unexplained v2.4.57 epidemic shift, the three
hardcoded values, the untested NSR-independence assumption, the chosen
configuration and the profiler bias — were never committed and are preserved
only in this entry and the 2.4.60 one above.

`test_calibrated_grid` runs each configuration cell's OWN cal_1 -> cal_2 ->
production instead of measuring every cell against one frozen table. 18 cells
(1000/3000/5000 x 1%/3% x R = R_long = 1.05/1.06/1.07), 250 tasks each, 4,500
total, counted from the pipeline's own builders. The pipeline hardcodes the
configuration the grid is choosing — population 1000, R_long 1.1, a 365-day
cal_2 window that ends before phi=1 at these configurations — so
`tests/cellconfig/sitecustomize.py` overrides them in every stage subprocess
via the import hook. Nothing in `scripts/` is modified.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Version 2.4.60 — 2026-10-05 — pietro gerletti

The same seed now reproduces; a task Slurm calls COMPLETED no longer hangs the
monitor; the progress bar is off; two append-only outputs stream to disk.

**Reproducibility.** Two runs of identical code on the same seed disagreed, every
time, in `individuals_data.csv` (a last-bit fitness value) and
`lineage_frequency.csv` (row order). `population.py` built `set(IH_lineages)`
from lineage NAMES, and Python randomises string hashing per process, so the
set's iteration order set the insertion order of `share_lineages_t` — hence the
row order written out and the summation order for fitness. Now
`sorted(set(...))`, matching `population_model.py:189`, which already did this.
Verified on a full 1095-day run with PYTHONHASHSEED deliberately unset: 7 of 7
files identical, where before the fix two differed. Cost measured at ~0.03%.
**Recalibration required**: no run produced before this commit reproduces
exactly on current code.

**The monitor hang.** profile_grid_#910 completed 116 of 120 tasks, wrote 120
profiles, and `sacct` reported every job COMPLETED — yet four `.completed`
signals were missing, so `left` never reached 0 and the poll loop ran for hours
against a frozen `.progress` snapshot with an empty queue.
`reconcile_terminated_tasks` only matched SLURM_TERMINAL_FAILURE_STATES, and
COMPLETED is deliberately not in that set. It is now reconciled as `.completed`,
not `.failed` — the job script exited 0, so the simulation ran and only the
signal write is missing; calling it failed would under-report the run. Both
signals are re-checked immediately before writing, since `stuck_paths` is built
thousands of stats earlier on a large grid.

**tqdm.** The progress bar redrew on every extrande event — 330k times in an
N=5000 run — and cost 18% of runtime under Slurm, writing to a log nobody reads.
Commented out; 26.74s -> 21.37s on an N=1000 run. Output verified byte-identical
with the hash seed pinned. The event counters it fed are kept: they are the
run's own accounting.

**Streamed outputs.** `lineage_frequency` and `simulation_trajectory` are
append-only and nothing reads them back mid-run, so they now go to
`<name>.csv.partial` row by row and are renamed on save. 555 MB and 47 MB
respectively in the worst N=5000 task of #910. Benchmarked at 1.9M rows:
accumulate-then-pandas 6.05s / 868 MB peak, streamed 4.58s / 12 MB, output
byte-identical — streaming is faster, not slower. The rename matters:
`check_completed_simulations.py:32` reads the presence of
`lineage_frequency.csv` as "this simulation finished". Two traps found by test:
pandas widens the t=0 row's integers into a float64 column and writes `0.0`,
and `csv.writer` defaults to `\r\n`; either alone changes every line.
`phylogenetic_data` is deliberately NOT streamed — its rows are read on the hot
path (`get_lineage_genome`) and mutated afterwards (`Total_infections`), so a
streamed copy would be stale, and `_phylo_name_map` holds every row anyway.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Version 2.4.59 — 2026-10-05 — pietro gerletti

Per-process memory and CPU profiling for Slurm tasks, with no change to
`simplicity/` or `scripts/`.

`simplicity/runners/slurm.py:243` passes the whole environment through sbatch
and Python imports `sitecustomize` at startup, so `tests/profiling` on
PYTHONPATH profiles every task. Each writes RSS, VmHWM, CPU time, extrande's
event counters and the measured bytes of all twelve growable Population
containers every 15s, plus sampled stacks at 0.25s. Every row is fsync'd: a task
killed for exceeding --mem dies on SIGKILL and never runs atexit, so the partial
curve is the whole diagnosis. Verified by killing a live run — last row at day
250, RSS 293 MB, 6,138 lineage rows.

**Read section 4 of its report as candidates, not percentages.** The stack
sampler is a Python thread calling `sys._current_frames()`, so it only samples
while holding the GIL, which the main thread releases during numpy calls and
I/O. It reported `consensus.py:add` at 19.5-30.7% of self time; direct timing
puts it at 0.30% of a full run — inflated roughly 90x. The tqdm finding from the
same report was confirmed independently against wall clock, so the bias is not
uniform. Direct timing of a 1095-day run: `population_model.infection` 7.3%,
`_update_states_matrix` 6.2%, `add_lineage` 5.9%, `update_lineage_frequency_t`
0.9%.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Version 2.4.58 — 2026-10-04 — pietro gerletti

`test_config_grid.py --analyse-only` takes the seed count from the run instead
of from its own flag.

Grid #906 was run with `--seeds 50` and analysed without repeating the flag, so
the report computed its expectations from the default 10 and printed
`simulations : 1100 of 900 (122%)` while marking three partial cells complete.
Section 0 exists to stop an incomplete report reading as trustworthy and it
mislabelled this one; 24% of the grid was present, not 122%.

New `seeds_on_disk` reads `n_seeds` from the experiment's own file, across
whichever part names exist, and the report says which number it used. A
mismatch is announced on stdout, and a total above 100% now prints an explicit
warning that the numbers are unsafe rather than a cheerful percentage.

Also recorded from that report: the losses are monotone in population --
N=1000 kept 57% of its simulations, N=2500 17%, N=5000 none of 1500, across two
separate attempts. That is the unbounded consensus snapshot, which v2.4.57
replaced: at N=5000 over 1095 days it reached millions of genome dicts at
~1,281 bytes each, well past the 4G task limit. The N=5000 row should be
runnable for the first time now.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Version 2.4.57 — 2026-10-04 — pietro gerletti

The consensus is accumulated incrementally instead of rescanned, and the
snapshot list is gone.

`get_consensus` rebuilt from the whole snapshot every 5 simulated days, so cost
grew with elapsed days x circulating lineages while the list itself grew
without bound. Run #3 at N=1000 reached 160,319 entries by day 1095 in
HIV_high; the grid task that sat at an identical progress value for 3.5 hours
was at N=5000 with 2,234 infected, where the list plausibly held two to three
million genome dicts. Measured, a snapshot entry costs ~1,281 bytes, so a
million of them is 1.3 GB against a 4 GB task limit.

The Bateman kernel is a difference of two pure exponentials in dt, so the
exponent separates and every weighted sum over history factorises into
e^(-k*t) times a quantity that does not depend on t. New
`consensus.ConsensusAccumulator` keeps that quantity per (position, base) for
each exponential, plus two scalars, and recovers the matrix at any t in
O(positions). Accumulators are held relative to a moving reference time,
rescaled at each evaluation, so stored exponents never exceed ~e^0.43 instead
of e^(k_a*1095) ~ 3e40. The layout mirrors build_weighted_consensus_matrix
exactly -- each entry adds to its own base row and subtracts from the reference
row, which is then seeded with the running total -- so a genome holding a
reference-valued base cancels in both implementations.

`population.consensus_snapshot` is replaced by `population.consensus`. Nothing
ever persisted the list (only consensus_sequences_t is written out), so it is
deleted rather than pruned. A day's lineages are now keyed by genome before
being added: lineages carrying byte-identical genomes contribute identically
and frequency enters linearly, so they merge into one accumulate.

`distance.DISTANCE_TOL` (1e-9) extends v2.4.48's clamp from "negative" to "in
the noise band". The centred distance is a cancellation that should give
exactly 0 for a lineage carrying the consensus, and in doubles it lands either
side of zero; the negative case was v2.4.48, and the positive case is just as
unstable -- a one-ulp change moves it by 100% relative. Measured on a real run,
distances are either 0 or >= 1e-3 with nothing in between, so the threshold has
six orders of magnitude of clearance and can only catch rounding.

EQUIVALENCE

The maths is identical, proven two ways. Both paths reimplemented in 60-digit
decimal arithmetic agree to 5.47e-59 relative -- the rounding floor of that
precision, i.e. no difference at all. In float64 the live run compares both at
every one of 184 rebuilds: max |dP| 1.8e-14, |d(delta)| 2.3e-13, consensus
sequence identical every time.

The argmax pipeline is byte-identical, 21 files of 21: hamming_iw returns an
integer, so last-bit matrix changes cannot reach the distance unless an argmax
flips, and none did.

The distribution pipeline's EPIDEMIC is byte-identical -- compartment counts
and final_time -- because propensities depend on counts, not fitness. Its
GENEALOGY is not. Fitness steers rng4.choice when an infection picks its
parent; hundreds of hosts carry near-identical fitness, so the selection CDF
has tightly spaced boundaries and a reordered sum eventually moves one past a
uniform draw. Measured: at day 53.01 one host inherits wt.7 instead of wt.0
(20->21 and 31->30 carriers) and everything downstream follows.

That is a property of the model, not of this change: the genome keying alone
would do it, as would a numpy upgrade on untouched code. The clamp does not
prevent it either -- it was added on the hypothesis that it would, which was
wrong; it is kept because returning 1.1e-16 where the answer is 0 is simply
incorrect. Byte-identity is not achievable for that pipeline and is not the
bar; the maths being provably identical is.

COST

Live, at N=1000 control with the snapshot reaching 8,648 entries: 23.70 ms per
rebuild rescanning against 1.39 ms accumulating, 17.1x, with the rescan
accounting for 24% of an 18-second run. The accumulator's cost is flat in
history, so the ratio grows with it -- roughly 390x at HIV_high's 160k entries.
Memory goes from unbounded to 1.88 MB with every one of the 4,967 positions
occupied.

Verification in `tests/test_consensus_accumulator.py` (a simple snapshot, a
1,095-day span, 219 rescalings against one, genome keying, and the live
both-paths run) and `tests/test_consensus_wiring.py` (argmax strict on every
file, distribution strict on the epidemic and reporting the genealogy).

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Version 2.4.56 — 2026-10-04 — pietro gerletti

`test_config_grid.py` submits the grid as several sequential arrays instead of
one, because one 4500-task array is refused outright.

v2.4.55 put the whole grid in a single array on the reasoning that this is what
Slurm arrays are for. sbatch rejected it: `squeue --me` came back empty and
`submit_simulations`' assert fired, which interpolates the entire environment
dict into its message and buries the actual sbatch error under pages of LMOD
and PATH. The release cap is irrelevant here -- `submit_simulations` queues the
WHOLE array at once with `--hold`, so all 4500 jobs hit the submit limit before
a single one is released.

New `--max-array-tasks` (default 900, the size cal_1 is known to submit
successfully; 0 restores a single array). `chunk_groups` splits the
`_scenario_groups` list so no array exceeds it, and they are submitted one
after another as `config_grid_part1`, `part2`, ... under one --exp-num, so only
one array is ever queued. At 50 seeds that is 5 arrays of 900.

Collection, timing and the `.failed` count now span every part:
`grid_params_files` globs both the single-array and the part names, and each
combination's output root is derived from its own parameters file rather than
assumed, so a grid split one way and re-analysed later still resolves.

The MaxArraySize pre-flight now checks the per-array size rather than the
grand total, and reports the `--max-array-tasks` value that would fit.

Verified locally: 90 groups chunk to 5 arrays of 900 at the default, 9 of 500
when asked, and 1 of 4500 at 0, with the task total preserved in every case.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Version 2.4.55 — 2026-10-04 — pietro gerletti

`test_config_grid.py` submits the whole grid as ONE Slurm array, and refuses
to present an incomplete report as a finished one.

The first run of it lost two thirds of the grid. `run_all` started one thread
per (cell, scenario) and fired 90 `sbatch` calls at once; Slurm refused most of
them, `submit_simulations`' assert raised, the worker caught it, and the
failures went to stdout only -- never into the report file. 2 of 18 cells came
back complete, 6 partial, 10 absent, and the decision table rendered as though
nothing was wrong. The cap split 90 ways also gave each surviving experiment
`200 // 90 = 2` concurrent seeds, so a third of the work took 6h53m.

The grid is now a single experiment. `build_groups` emits one all-scalar
`_scenario_groups` entry per (cell, scenario) -- the same mechanism cal_1 uses
to submit its 30 groups as 900 tasks in one array -- so 18 cells x 5 scenarios
becomes 90 parameter combinations and 4500 tasks under one `--array`. No
threads, no cap arithmetic, and Slurm schedules it. Group scalars are layered
over the pipeline's own `build_exp_scenario_settings` output rather than
rebuilt, with population and starting infections added on top.

Because one experiment now holds every cell, a combination is identified from
its own written parameters file -- population, initial infected, R, and
(long_shedders_ratio, tau_3_long) mapped back to a scenario -- rather than from
a directory name. `02_Simulation_parameters/<name>.json` and
`04_Output/<name>/` share a basename, which is what makes that reliable.

New section 0, printed before anything else: cells complete, simulations
measured against expected, `.failed` count, and a line per partial or absent
cell. When any cell is incomplete it says in full that the tables below are not
comparable and that no configuration should be chosen from them. Incomplete
cells are also flagged inline in the decision table. The previous report was
authoritative-looking and two-thirds empty; this is the fix for that, not for
the submission bug.

Before submitting, the task count is checked against the cluster's
MaxArraySize read from `scontrol show config`, and the run aborts with the
largest `--seeds` that would fit rather than letting sbatch refuse the array.

Verified locally without simulating: 18 cells x 5 scenarios produce exactly 90
settings with no list-valued group entries (so no accidental cartesian
expansion), all pass `check_parameters_names`, every one maps back to its
scenario, and section 0 renders the all-absent case correctly.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Version 2.4.54 — 2026-10-03 — pietro gerletti

New `tests/test_config_grid.py`: sweeps population x starting infections x R
to find a configuration the next production run can stand on.

`test_r_sweep.py` answered its question and uncovered a third constraint. At
N = 1000 the usable R window is narrow -- 1.05/1.06 fade out, 1.07 saturates --
and N = 5000 widened it to 88-90% completion with no saturation. But phi =
(diagnosed + recovered)/N then needed 5000 hosts exposed from the same 50
seeds, so it did not reach 1 until day 450-900 of 1095, leaving almost nothing
to measure after the burn-in opened. Fixing fade-out broke the window.

So a configuration has to clear three bars at once, and the grid reports all
three per cell: completion against the horizon, the WEAKEST single scenario's
completion (an uneven cell reintroduces exactly the survival bias that makes
scenarios incomparable), saturation count, the day phi reaches 1, and the new
`window` metric -- days between phi = 1 and the run ending, which is what is
actually left to measure. Run #3 is carried as a reference row: 48% complete,
phi at day 226, a 786-day window.

Defaults: populations 1000/2500/5000, i0 fractions 1/3/5% (production is
50/1000 = 5%), R 1.06/1.07, 18 cells. Task count, wave count, a wall-clock
estimate and the resulting precision all print before anything is submitted:
900 tasks and +/-7 points at 10 seeds, 4500 and +/-3 at 50.

That precision line exists because the previous sweep was over-read, here and
in the discussion around it: at 10 seeds a cell pools 50 simulations, so 88%
against 90% is one simulation and the per-scenario spread is noise. The pooled
1.06-vs-1.07 difference held; the scenario-by-scenario pattern did not.

Section 5 deliberately does not rank the cells. Completion alone is not the
criterion -- a cell can reach the horizon often and still open its burn-in too
late to measure anything -- so the report states the three bars in priority
order and leaves the choice.

Verified with `--analyse-only` against run #3: the reference row renders with
every new column, including the window metric.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Version 2.4.53 — 2026-10-03 — pietro gerletti

`test_r_sweep.py`: initial infected is held at production's 50 regardless of
population size, reversing v2.4.52's scaling. Population size is then the only
thing that moves between the sweeps, which is what the comparison is for; at
5000 hosts that means 1% starting prevalence against production's 5%, by
choice. `--i0` still overrides.

Dropped the warning about --slurm-mem at larger populations: 4G is enough.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Version 2.4.52 — 2026-10-03 — pietro gerletti

`test_r_sweep.py` sweeps population size as well as R.

The first sweep answered its question and killed its own premise. R = 1.05
lifted completion from 48% to 68%; R = 1.10 produced 0 of 50, every run ending
on `susceptibles == 0` with a median peak of 1000 infected -- the whole
population at once, burnt out by day ~340. The usable window between
stochastic fade-out and susceptible exhaustion is narrow, and that is a
property of a 1000-host population rather than of R.

(A correction: v2.4.51's framing said saturation was unlikely because the
reservoir tops susceptibles back up on every diagnosis. It refills at the
DIAGNOSIS rate, so once infection outruns diagnosis the susceptibles drain
anyway. The R = 1.10 column is that being wrong.)

New `--population-size` (default 1000) and `--i0`, which by default scales the
initial infected with the population so starting prevalence -- and with it the
chance of early stochastic death -- matches production's 50/1000. Overrides
reach the simulation through `with_overrides`, which wraps
`build_exp_scenario_settings` rather than rebuilding the parameter dict.

`measure` now takes the population it must normalise phi by. It was hardcoded
to 1000, which at 5000 hosts would have reported phi = 1 at a fifth of the real
day and moved every burn-in number without any sign that it had. The baseline
column keeps 1000, the swept runs use theirs, and the header says so:
completion rates stay comparable across populations, absolute counts do not.

Experiment names carry the population (`rtest_<R>_N<pop>_<scenario>`) so sweeps
at different sizes cannot collide; the report file does too.

Timing is now reported per R as well as pooled. The pooled figure from the
first sweep (median 51s) was dragged down by R = 1.10 runs that all died by day
~340 -- the cheapest configuration was the one nobody would run. The projection
note now says to read the slowest R.

Warns when the population is above 1000 and `--slurm-mem` is still the default
4G, which was sized on production's 1000 hosts.

Verified with `--analyse-only` against run #3: the baseline column reproduces
unchanged at its own population, and the new header, per-R timing and naming
all render.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Version 2.4.51 — 2026-10-03 — pietro gerletti

`tests/test_r_sweep.py`: does raising R stop the epidemics fizzling out?

Run #3 reached the horizon in only 238 of 500 production simulations under the
distributional consensus -- 33% for control, 79% for HIV_high. Almost none died
out (1-3 per scenario) and NONE saturated. They stopped on extrande's third
condition, `t > 60 and infectious == 0`: a few hosts still flagged infected,
none in an infectious compartment, so transmission was over. At R = 1.03 in a
population of 1000 that is expected behaviour, not a failure -- and it means
any "ran to completion" filter selects a biased subset, which is how the ten
seeds for the tree comparison were chosen.

The test reuses run #3's frozen table unchanged and runs the same five
scenarios at R = R_long = 1.05 and 1.10, 10 seeds each, distributional
consensus only -- equalising R and R_long so infection duration is the only
thing separating the cohorts. 100 tasks, written to
`rtest_<R>_<scenario>_#903` so nothing touches real data. All ten experiments
submit together, splitting the concurrency cap as the production dispatcher
does and restoring it afterwards.

Reports, to stdout and `Data/rtest_report_#903.txt`: completion against the
horizon with run #3 as the baseline column; why the rest stopped, classified by
extrande's real conditions in their real order (so saturation appearing is
visible as the opposite failure); peak infected and the day phi saturates,
which also bears on figure 4's burn-in; timing; and a verdict.

Timing measures per-task wall clock from the .started/.completed signal mtimes
rather than the sweep's own elapsed time, which carries queue wait, then
projects a full pipeline as ceil(tasks/cap) waves at the p95 task. Measured at
the swept R on purpose: higher R means bigger epidemics and slower tasks, so it
projects the run actually being considered. Against HIV_high #3 the per-task
median is 2m28s and p95 6m38s, projecting ~1h19m for a full pipeline at 100
seeds under a cap of 200.

The NSR in that table was fitted at R = 1.03 and is wrong for the swept values.
Deliberate, and stated at the top of the output: fade-out is a property of
transmission, not of the mutation clock. Completion rates only -- no clocks, no
clades.

Verified locally without running any simulation: `--analyse-only` against the
existing #3 data reproduces the baseline column exactly (33/39/43/79/44 per
scenario, 238/500 overall, phi medians 203/208/217/310/204), and `task_seconds`
plus `projection` were exercised against real signal files.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Version 2.4.50 — 2026-10-01 — pietro gerletti

Submitting over an earlier attempt's signal files now refuses, and the
"submitted N" line says what actually happens.

A cal_long #1 run on hpc-login02 reported, before anything had run:

    submitted 900 seeded simulations
    SimulationsStatus(total=900, submitted=900, released=900, left=0,
                      pending=0, started=900, running=0, completed=900, failed=0)

and then died two stages later on "No valid long-calibration data found". The
900-task array sat (JobHeldUser) in the queue the whole time.

`poll_simulations_status` reads the `.submitted`/`.released`/`.started`/
`.completed`/`.failed` files next to each seeded-params file, NOT Slurm. A set
left behind by an earlier attempt at the same `--exp-num` therefore decides the
run before it starts: with `.completed` present, `left = submitted -
(completed+failed)` is 0 on the first poll, the `while ... left > 0` loop never
executes, and `release_simulations` -- which is only reached from inside that
loop -- is never called. Nothing in `run_experiment` clears those files;
`create_directories` and the parameter rewrite leave them, and a Data tree
copied between machines carries them without the outputs.

New `find_stale_signals` / `raise_on_stale_signals`, called at the top of
`submit_simulations`, BEFORE sbatch, so a refused run leaves no held array
behind. It refuses on any leftover signal, not just `.completed` -- a stray
`.started` miscounts the run just as surely -- and it deletes nothing: which of
two runs matters is not something this code can know. The message names the
counts per suffix, says plainly that nothing was submitted, and gives both ways
out (a clean `--exp-num`, or the exact `find ... -delete`).

The submission line read as "N are running now", which it never was. It now
says:

    [2026-10-01 20:25:58] probe: queued 900 seeded simulations as one held
    Slurm array; at most 200 released to run at a time

That is the actual mechanism: the whole array goes to Slurm at once with
`--hold`, and `release_simulations` lets them go a capped number at a time.
Submitting 900 against a cap of 200 was never a bug.

With that line now worth keeping, both pipelines' `_NOISE_PATTERNS` are empty
-- v2.4.47 already stopped dropping `SimulationsStatus` -- so the list and
`_is_slurm_monitor_noise` are removed and `run_stage` keeps every non-blank
line.

Verification in `tests/test_stale_signals.py`: a clean directory submits; the
hpc-login02 shape (all four signals on every seed) refuses with the counts in
the message; a single stray `.started` refuses too; and with signals present
`submit_simulations` raises without calling sbatch and without writing any new
`.submitted`. `tests/test_status_reporting.py` asserts the new wording.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Version 2.4.49 — 2026-10-01 — pietro gerletti

Sequencing moves out of the simulation, scenarios submit together, and the
pipeline packs its own results.

SEQUENCING (UPSTREAM_TODO item 1, **RECAL**)

`population_model.diagnosis` no longer draws `rng6.uniform(0,1) < seq_rate`. It
records `t_diagnosis` instead, and the run writes the COMPLETE record -- every
diagnosed host, every distinct genome it carried -- which is what
`sequencing_rate = 1.0` produced before and what both calibration stages
already asked for. `sequencing_rate` is gone from `settings_manager`, `runme`
and both pipeline configs; `seq_rate` is gone from `SIDR_propensities` and
`extrande`; `rng6` is gone, with `Population.sequencing_data`.

New `simplicity/sequencing.py` builds these datasets after the fact.
`complete_sequencing_rows(population)` is what the writer now calls;
`reconstruct_sequencing_data(individuals_data, phylogenetic_data,
sequencing_ratio, seed)` rebuilds one at ANY ratio from a finished run's CSVs,
so a ratio is an argument rather than a reason to simulate again. The draw is
per INDIVIDUAL, never per row: a host is sequenced or not, and then all of its
distinct genomes are read, matching the mechanism it replaces. It refuses, with
an explanatory error, on output that predates `t_diagnosis`.

The calibration path is untouched: it reads `sequencing_data_regression.csv`,
which is still written, now from the complete record.

PARALLEL SCENARIO SUBMISSION

`impact_long_shedders_unbound_exp.dispatch_all` submits every scenario at once.
`run_seeded_simulations` blocks until its own experiment finishes, so five
scenarios ran strictly one after another -- 30 seeds in flight at a time
against a cap of 200, five times over. They now go together in threads (the
work is subprocess calls and sleeps, so the GIL is never the constraint), with
the cap SPLIT between them -- 40 each of 200, not 200 each -- and restored
afterwards. The stage should now take about as long as its slowest scenario
rather than the sum of all five. `--sequential` restores the old behaviour;
serial/multiprocessing runners are unaffected. A failing scenario is reported
alongside the others and then re-raised, so the pipeline still stops.

`print_simulations_status` now names the experiment, because five polling loops
interleave: `[2026-10-01 12:57:15] my_experiment_#3 SimulationsStatus(...)`.
The `submitted N seeded simulations` line names it too, so both runners' noise
patterns lost their `$` anchor.

PACKING

The pipeline ends by submitting `package_simplicity_data.sh Data _#<exp_num>`
to Slurm, so `Data_Export/*.tar.xz` is ready to download without a second
command. The glob takes every directory from the run -- both calibrations,
setup data, every scenario of every consensus mode, both sanity dirs -- with no
bound/unbound assumption and no run number written out. `--no-compress` skips
it. A packing failure is reported, never raised: the data is on disk either
way.

`package_simplicity_data.sh` now keeps `slurm/` when
`SIMPLICITY_KEEP_SLURM_LOGS=1`, and the pipeline sets it. Its STEP 1 deleted
those directories in place on the real `Data/`, and they are the only record of
why a task failed -- both of today's diagnoses (a truncated pickle, a numpy
ValueError) came out of them. `--drop-slurm-logs` restores the old behaviour.

VERIFICATION

`tests/test_sequencing_removal.py` compares two runs of the same three seeds
across the change: `final_time`, `lineage_frequency`, `phylogenetic_data` and
`simulation_trajectory` are byte-identical for every seed, and
`individuals_data` differs only by the added `t_diagnosis` column -- every
pre-existing column holds the same values, 741/721/895 individuals. rng6 was
drawn nowhere else and was created last from `seeds_generator`, so rng3/4/5 are
unchanged. The old sampled file is a strict subset of the new complete one with
identical sequences for every host it recorded (3 sampled hosts -> 36 in the
complete record, 1 -> 40, 2 -> 66), and `reconstruct_sequencing_data` at ratio
1.0 reproduces the written file row for row, is reproducible from its seed, and
never sequences a host partially.

`tests/test_parallel_dispatch.py`: all five scenarios in flight at once,
`--sequential` holds it to one, non-Slurm runners stay sequential, the cap
divides to 40 and is restored between calls (a bug this test found -- it was
compounding), failures are collected and re-raised, and `compress_results`
invokes the packager with the right suffix, `--slurm`, and the log-keeping
variable.

