## Version 2.4.44 — 2026-09-30 — pietro gerletti

Fixed a startup race on the precomputed matrix-exponential cache that killed
nine tasks of the unbound production array (`26554931_16` through `_24`, all
`FAILED 1:0` at 00:00:10).

`Host._load_or_precompute_exponentials` wrote the table with a plain
`open(file_path, "wb")` on a path shared by every concurrent job
(`output_manager.get_procomputed_matrix_table_filepath` resolves to `Data/`,
with no experiment scoping). `open(..., "wb")` truncates before `pickle.dump`
writes, so for the length of that window the file exists at zero bytes. A peer
task reaching the `os.path.exists` branch in that window loaded an empty file
and died on `EOFError: Ran out of input`.

`Population.__init__` builds two Hosts — `standard` on `tau_3` and
`long_shedder` on `tau_3_long` — so each task touches two such files and gets
two chances to lose. In the observed failures the standard host wrote its table
successfully (the `.out` ends with its "Saved 300 matrix exponentials" line) and
the long shedder then crashed on the other file at `population.py:177`.

The write now goes to `<file_path>.<pid>.tmp` followed by `os.replace`, which is
atomic within a filesystem, so a reader sees either no file or a complete one.
The read branch additionally catches `EOFError` and `UnpicklingError` and falls
through to recompute, which recovers from any zero-length file an earlier
version left behind. Losers of the race recompute in ~10 s and replace the
target with byte-identical content.

No recalibration: the table's contents and every downstream number are
unchanged. The failures were at construction, before any output was written, so
no completed seed is affected — the nine lost grid points simply need rerunning.

Verification in `tests/test_exp_table_race.py`: cold write leaves no `.tmp`
behind, a warm read reproduces the table exactly, a zero-byte and a
mid-stream-truncated cache both recover, and 16 processes released on a common
start barrier against one shared path all return the same 300-entry table. The
zero-byte case was replayed against the pre-fix body and reproduces the
production traceback verbatim (`EOFError: Ran out of input`).

