## Version 2.4.23 — 2026-08-25 — pietro gerletti

Found while investigating the k_v A/B test's OOM kills: `Population.
get_lineage_genome` did a linear scan over `self.phylogenetic_data`
(`next(d['Genome'] for d in self.phylogenetic_data if ...)`) even though
`self._phylo_name_map` already indexes the same rows by lineage name in
O(1) and is kept correctly in sync on every append (`mutations.py:439`).
Called twice per mutation event from inside the mutation loop itself
(`mutations.py:214`, `mutations.py:425`), and `phylogenetic_data`'s length
*is* the cumulative mutation count for the run -- so the total cost of
just these lookups was O(mutations²), not O(mutations). Swapped the scan
for the existing dict lookup; verified with `tests/test_local_runme.py`
(long-shedder scenario, mutations + sequencing + phylogenetic-data output
all exercised) -- output unchanged, ran clean.

* `Population.get_lineage_genome`: `next(...)` linear scan -> O(1)
  `self._phylo_name_map[lineage_name]['Genome']` lookup. No other code
  changes required; `_phylo_name_map` was already correct.

