## Version 2.4.20 — 2026-08-20 — pietro gerletti

Real pipeline run #1 (2.4.19's values) got past Stage 1 cleanly -- cal_1's
fit worked exactly as validated (SOT NSR=0.086, HIV_low/high NSR=0.062,
edge_case NSR=0.117, all inside the widened 0.01-0.5 range) -- but Stage 2
hit two new problems.

* `simplicity/dir_manager.py`: `SIMPLICITY_SLURM_MEM` default `3G` -> `4G`.
  Run #1 had 284 OOM failures across Stage 1+2 (~18% of ~1600 tasks,
  `reconcile_terminated_tasks` correctly caught and marked them `.failed`
  rather than hanging) -- likely the much wider `cal1_long_nsr` range
  (long-shedder NSR now up to 0.5, was 0.002) means more accumulated
  mutations per genome over the 3-year run, pushing memory past the old
  default.
* New `tests/test_long_shedder_std_nsr_grid.py`: Stage 2 counterpart to
  `test_long_shedder_r_long_grid.py`. Run #1's Stage 2 aborted -- SOT's
  standard-NSR fit came back essentially flat (R²=0.0003) within
  `NSR_RANGES['cal2_standard_nsr']`'s OLD range (1e-5-3e-4), correctly
  triggering the `NSR_SANITY_MAX` guard rather than returning garbage.
  `control` (no long shedders) fit fine in that same run (0.00019, within
  range) -- so this is specific to scenarios WITH long shedders: Stage 1's
  now much higher calibrated long-NSR (0.06-0.12) injects far more
  diversity into the mixed population than before, apparently swamping the
  standard individuals' own signal within the old narrow range. Same class
  of "range doesn't bracket target" problem as Stage 1, on the side that
  was never re-checked. Uses Stage 1's real fitted long-NSR values from
  run #1 as fixed per-scenario inputs (not re-derived); sweeps 3 NSR anchor
  points (default 1e-5-0.05, decoupled from
  `NSR_RANGES['cal2_standard_nsr']` the same way the Stage 1 test is
  decoupled from `NSR_RANGES['cal1_long_nsr']`) across all 5 scenarios
  (control included, as a sanity reference). Same recap-file + combined-
  figure + zip output pattern as the Stage 1 test.

