## Version 2.4.17 — 2026-08-18 — pietro gerletti

Reworked `tests/test_long_shedder_r_long_grid.py` based on the first HPC
run's results (test #1, edge_case, R_long in {1,1.5,2,4} x
infected_individuals_at_start in {10,50,100}, 10-point NSR sweep,
final_time=3yr). That run showed two things clearly: (1) the sequence-
accumulation problem chased locally the night before was specific to
testing at final_time=365 -- at the real 3-year duration, edge_case gets
plenty of data even at the default starting-infected count, so that axis
wasn't worth continuing to sweep; (2) the actual dominant problem is
`NSR_RANGES['cal1_long_nsr']` (0.0005-0.002) never getting anywhere near
the target OSR (0.00205) -- R² stayed poor (0.002-0.243) across the entire
grid because the sampled range sits on a nearly-flat part of the
OSR-vs-NSR curve.

* Swept axes changed: now tests every long-shedder production scenario (by
  **unique** `tau_3_long` -- HIV_low/HIV_high share a duration in this
  isolated context and would otherwise just duplicate identical runs) x
  R_long in {1.0, 1.3}, instead of one fixed scenario x R_long in
  {1,1.5,2,4} x starting-infected in {10,50,100}.
* NSR reduced from a 10-point sweep to 3 anchor points (min/mid/max of
  `NSR_RANGES['cal1_long_nsr']`) -- this test is now a bracketing check
  (does the sampled range reach the target OSR?), not a curve-shape scan.
* `infected_individuals_at_start` fixed at 10 (no longer swept).
* New outputs: a plain-text recap (`Data/tests/test_cal_1_out_#{test_n}.txt`)
  with per-cell fit stats (`A`, `B`, R², n, mean final_time) *and* raw mean
  OSR at each of the 3 NSR points (so bracketing can be read directly,
  without trusting a fit that only has 3 points to work with), plus the
  combined grid figure (3 scenario rows x 2 R_long columns, shared axes) --
  both zipped together into `Data/{experiment_name}_artifacts.zip`.

