## Version 2.4.16 — 2026-08-18 — pietro gerletti

Local testing/tuning of cal_1's isolated long-shedder context under the new
directly-specified R_long design (2.4.15), plus a new standalone HPC
exploration tool.

* `CAL1_ISOLATED_FIXED_PARAMS['population_size']` 5000 -> 1000 (the 5000
  bump from 2.4.14 was overkill once R_long stopped being formula-derived).
* `SCENARIOS['R_long']`: SOT and HIV_low/HIV_high raised 1.0 -> 1.3 --
  confirmed via 30 local multiprocessing simulations (10 seeds each) that
  1.0 leaves a ~1-in-3 chance of stochastic extinction before 365 days
  (expected near-critical behavior, not a bug), while 1.3 survives 10/10
  for both groups. edge_case stays at 1.0 (already 10/10 at that value).
* Running the real `cal_1.py` end-to-end at these values (150 local sims,
  0 failures) surfaced a *second*, distinct problem beyond survival:
  edge_case's simulations reach the full 365 days but only accumulate
  3-8 sequenced diagnoses each (vs `min_seq=30`) -- tau_3_long=350.23 is
  almost as long as final_time=365, so individuals barely complete a full
  infectious cycle within the window. Confirmed raising R_long does NOT
  fix this (transmission rate isn't the bottleneck); raising
  `infected_individuals_at_start` (more parallel starting cohorts) helps
  substantially (3-8 -> 20-39 seqs at 60) but at 60 it also pushes SOT into
  early depletion (most seeds now end via "No susceptibles left" well
  before 365 days) -- a genuine three-way tension between edge_case's
  sequencing needs, SOT's survival, and a single shared parameter. Not yet
  resolved -- `infected_individuals_at_start` stays at its prior value (10)
  pending further tuning; edge_case's calibration fit is still unusable as
  of this commit. SOT and HIV_low's fits are also currently poor (R² near
  0 / weak) at only 5 diagnostic seeds/NSR-point -- not yet determined
  whether that's genuine NSR-range mismatch or just statistical noise from
  the small diagnostic seed count.
* New `tests/test_long_shedder_r_long_grid.py`: standalone HPC tool (not
  part of the impact_long_shedders pipeline) that sweeps the isolated
  long-shedder NSR calibration over a grid of R_long (1/1.5/2/4) x
  starting-infected-count (10/50/100), for one tau_3_long borrowed from a
  chosen production scenario (`--scenario`, default edge_case), at
  final_time=3 years. Produces one combined figure (R_long x starting-count
  grid, shared axes, R²/mean-final-time annotated per cell) to visually
  compare calibration curves before picking real defaults. Reuses
  `NSR_RANGES['cal1_long_nsr']` and `CAL1_ISOLATED_FIXED_PARAMS['R']` from
  `impact_long_shedders_config.py` rather than duplicating them.

