## Version 2.4.12 — 2026-08-17 — pietro gerletti

Two correctness fixes surfaced by inspecting run #2's calibration/sanity
plots and the resulting HIV_high/edge_case dynamics, plus CLI exposure of
the parameters they touch.

* **R_long now has a single source of truth and actually matches its
  intent.** `sp['R_long']`/`CAL1_R_LONG_WEEKLY_RATE` were two disconnected
  "weekly rate" proxies, neither tied to the R the standard population
  actually ran with -- beta_long ended up ~1.4-1.5x beta_standard by
  accident. New `simplicity.settings_manager.compute_r_long(R, tau_2,
  tau_3, tau_3_long, multiplier=1.0)` is the shared formula:
  `R_long = R * multiplier * (tau_2+tau_3_long) / (tau_2+tau_3)`, so
  beta_long == beta_standard * multiplier exactly. `multiplier` is a new
  per-scenario `beta_multiplier` field on `SCENARIOS` (defaults 1.0 for
  all five). `R_long` is no longer a stored default anywhere -- removed
  from `standard_values.json`/`write_standard_parameters_values()`;
  `read_settings_and_write_simulation_parameters` computes it via
  `compute_r_long` whenever an experiment doesn't override it explicitly.
  This required fixing `check_parameters_names`, which validated
  parameter names against `standard_values.json`'s keys (so dropping
  `R_long` from there would have broken every explicit override) -- it now
  validates against `parameter_specs.json` instead, correctly decoupling
  "valid parameter name" from "has a static default".
* **`infect_long_shedder` recruitment probability now tracks
  `long_shedders_ratio`** (`population_model.py`) instead of a hardcoded
  1% literal -- every scenario used to recruit long shedders at the same
  fixed rate regardless of its configured ratio, making e.g. `HIV_high`'s
  12% cap largely aspirational. Side effect: `CAL1_ISOLATED_FIXED_PARAMS`'s
  `long_shedders_ratio=1.0` ("100% LS" isolated context) now actually
  behaves as advertised.
* R is now CLI-configurable per stage instead of a hardcoded constant:
  `impact_long_shedders_cal_1.py --R` (default 1.1, was
  `CAL1_ISOLATED_FIXED_PARAMS['R']=1.0`), `impact_long_shedders_cal_2.py
  --R` (default 1.03, was `USER_FIXED_PARAMS['R']=1.05`) -- both also gain
  `--beta-multiplier-long`, applied uniformly across every scenario. The
  pipeline orchestrator exposes the same as `--r-cal1`/`--r-cal2`/
  `--beta-multiplier-long`. Production (`exp.py`) now reads R from the
  frozen `nsr_calibration_table.csv` (new `R` column, written by cal_2)
  instead of the raw `USER_FIXED_PARAMS` default, so it always matches
  whatever R cal_2 actually calibrated against, even under a CLI override.
* Recalibration required: any experiment run before this fix used the old,
  uncorrected R_long and recruitment probability.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Version 2.4.11 — 2026-08-14 — pietro gerletti

Follow-up to 2.4.10: reviewing run #1's calibration-fit and sanity plots
(the first real run with the archiving feature) surfaced a plotting bug in
`impact_long_shedders_calibration_std_nsr_#1_std_nsr_calibration_fit.png`
and prompted a broader look at `plot_sot_sanity_regressions.py`'s design
and filtering, plus a round of population/resource parameter tuning.

* Parameter tuning: `SIMPLICITY_SLURM_MEM` 2G -> 3G
  (`simplicity/dir_manager.py`); `infected_individuals_at_start` 100 -> 50
  in both `CAL1_ISOLATED_FIXED_PARAMS` and `USER_FIXED_PARAMS`
  (`impact_long_shedders_config.py`), R values unchanged; standard seed
  count defaults 25 -> 50 (`impact_long_shedders_cal_1.py --seeds`,
  pipeline's `--cal-seeds`/`--exp-seeds`).
* `impact_long_shedders_cal_2.py`'s Stage 2 plot: added `plt.xscale('log')`.
  `control` (no long shedders) sweeps a 5x wider NSR range than the other
  four scenarios by design (mixed populations need a lower own-NSR to hit
  the same target OSR, since standard individuals sit downstream of
  long-shedder-injected diversity) -- on a shared linear axis that crushed
  the other four into an illegible sliver. `plot_calibration_step_1.py`
  already used log-x for the same kind of plot; this brings cal_2's
  combined plot in line with that precedent.
* NSR ranges narrowed further: `cal2_standard_nsr` unified to the same
  `{min: 1e-05, max: 3e-04, steps: 10}` for all 5 scenarios (previously
  control alone used a 5x wider max); `cal1_long_nsr` narrowed
  `0.0005-0.005` -> `0.0005-0.002` (both the live JSON and
  `impact_long_shedders_config.DEFAULT_NSR_RANGES`).
* `plot_sot_sanity_regressions.py`, three changes:
  - Both loaders now apply the same `min_len=100`/`min_seq=30` gate
    `cal_2.py`'s own calibration fits use (previously unfiltered), so this
    sanity check validates the same population of seeds calibration was
    actually fit on instead of pooling in short/sparse seeds it excluded.
  - Left panel redesigned: standard individuals' global clock (target +
    fit + scatter) plus a black "mixed" line fit on ALL individuals pooled
    together (what unstratified surveillance data would show) on the
    primary axis; long-shedder intra-host clock (target + fit, no scatter)
    overlaid on a secondary y-axis with its own target, single combined
    legend. Right panel (intra-host detail) unchanged.
  - Restructured from 4 separate per-scenario PNGs into ONE combined 4x2
    grid (`--scenarios`, one row per long-shedder scenario, default
    SOT/HIV_low/HIV_high/edge_case) with every axis in the grid -- left
    primary, left secondary/twin, and the whole right column -- sharing
    one unified y-limit, and x-limits shared per column, so scenarios are
    directly visually comparable on one figure.
* Cascading pipeline changes to match the single combined plot:
  `submit_sanity_plot.sh` now submits ONE job instead of one per scenario
  (dropped the `<scenario>` arg; `--time` bumped 30min -> 2h since one job
  now does sequentially what 4 parallel jobs used to split).
  `run_impact_long_shedders_pipeline.py`'s `submit_sanity_plots` no longer
  reads the calibration table to loop over scenarios (dropped the now-
  unused `pandas` import entirely); `write_artifacts_archive` now expects
  the single combined `..._sanity_#{exp_num}_all_scenarios_global_vs_
  intrahost.png` instead of one per scenario.
* Verified locally (project conda env, no cluster needed): built synthetic
  experiment-output trees matching the real file schema (`final_time.csv`,
  `sequencing_data_regression.csv`, `individuals_data.csv`,
  `phylogenetic_data.csv` with correct sparse `(position, base)` genome
  tuples) and ran the real plotting script end-to-end -- confirmed the
  min_len/min_seq filter correctly excludes a deliberately-short seed from
  every fit, the twin-axis/merged-legend left panel renders correctly, and
  the 4-scenario combined grid renders with one true shared y-scale across
  all 8 axes. Separately mocked `squeue` responses to confirm the
  pipeline's single-job dispatch/wait/archive flow still works end-to-end
  post-refactor.

